<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palatine Chapel, Aachen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palatine Chapel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> is the octagonal chapel that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> built for his palace around 800, modelled on Byzantine churches such as San Vitale in Ravenna. Charlemagne was buried there in 814, and German kings were crowned there for six centuries. It forms the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral">Aachen Cathedral</a>, among the first sites on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palatine-chapel-aachen.jpg" alt="Palatine Chapel, Aachen" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lokilech · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachener_Dom_Oktogon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
